{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has one obligation: the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill is the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code. Under `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, a home means the topic is stated in full in that skill and in no other.\n\nThe test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_bootstrap_skill_is_home_of_deriving_an_initial_requirement_set]` covers this in two parts:\n- **Content in the home skill.** It reads the bootstrap `SKILL.md` and requires the sections `## Inventories`, `## Proposing requirements`, `## Tagging` and `## Report`. The `section` helper asserts that each heading exists. It then calls `assert_home`, which uses `assert_phrases` and `assert_text` to require that the bootstrap skill, including its references, contains each marker returned by `home_markers`. Those markers are \"every first-party runnable test or test case\", \"## Proposing requirements\" and \"Independent recount:\". A missing marker fails the assertion.\n- **Exclusivity.** `assert_home` loops over every other skill in `SKILLS` (`habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-refactor`). For each one it asserts that the skill's collapsed text, `SKILL.md` plus `references/`, does not contain any of the bootstrap markers. If another skill restated the topic, the test would fail with a message that names the skill and the marker.\n\nA plausible violation is a bootstrap skill that lacks the derivation content, or another skill that duplicates it. The test would reject both.\n\nThe word \"full\" in \"stated in full\" is only approximated by marker phrases and section headings. That is the same approach the project's other home tests use, and the text doesn't require more. The general \"exactly one home\" rule belongs to the cited `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, which has its own tests. I found no gap.",
  "references": "b02e72a8f64a2f9a1f68893267db6677c2ecf07ab8202182d024a68119169ae3",
